  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ denote the weight of the blade, and let $Y$ denote the weight of the rubber. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(X+Y>128).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(80,31)$ and $Y∼N(45,50),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(80+45,31+50) \\\\  & ∼N(125,81).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =125$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y>128),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & =P(Z>\\frac{128-125}{9}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.33) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.33) \\\\  & =Φ(0.33)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.33)≈0.6293.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>128) & ≈1-Φ(0.33) \\\\  & ≈1-0.6293 \\\\  & ≈0.3707.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
